{"acceptedResponses":["D"],"answer":"D","bank":"amc","batch":"NA","choiceOrder":["A","B","C","D","E"],"choices":{"A":"\\(18\\)","B":"\\(21\\)","C":"\\(24\\)","D":"\\(27\\)","E":"\\(30\\)"},"choicesType":"text","domain":[],"editorialNote":"","figureSources":{},"id":"1964_AMC_12_Q11","knowledgeGraph":{"assessedIds":["cnm_h042","cnm_088"],"contentHash":"f9ad2bf6c335f1c8123b200d57851b80079c2441fe981f78cc94d26cb77ce3ce","flags":[],"reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"substitution","label":"Equate exponents and substitute","methodDirectNodeIds":{"substitution":["cnm_089"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_089"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"elimination","label":"Equate exponents and eliminate","methodDirectNodeIds":{"elimination":["cnm_090"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_090"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_088","cnm_042"],"id":"common-bases-and-substitution","label":"Rewrite with common bases","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-bases-and-substitution":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042","cnm_089"]},"nodeIds":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042","cnm_089","cnm_088","cnm_042"]}],"status":"mapped","supportIds":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_042"],"version":"1.0.0"},"level":"AMC 12","number":11,"question":"Given \\(2^x=8^{y+1}\\) and \\(9^y=3^{x-9},\\) find the value of \\(x+y.\\)","questionType":"text","rejectedAnswers":[],"responseType":"choice","schemaVersion":"3.0","skill":[],"solution":"The numbers \\(8\\) and \\(9\\) are powers of \\(2\\) and \\(3\\), respectively. Rewriting them makes the exponents directly comparable:\n\\[\n2^x=2^{3(y+1)},\n\\qquad 3^{2y}=3^{x-9}.\n\\]\nFor a fixed base greater than \\(1\\), distinct real exponents give distinct powers. Therefore these equations are equivalent to\n\\[\nx=3y+3,\n\\qquad 2y=x-9.\n\\]\nThe second equation gives \\(x=2y+9\\). Substituting this into the first eliminates \\(x\\):\n\\[\n2y+9=3y+3,\n\\qquad y=6.\n\\]\nThen \\(x=2(6)+9=21\\), so\n\\[\nx+y=21+6=\\boxed{27}.\n\\]\nThe answer is D.","solutionAvailability":"present","solutionMethods":[{"directNodeIds":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042","cnm_089"],"explanation":"The numbers \\(8\\) and \\(9\\) are powers of \\(2\\) and \\(3\\), respectively. Rewriting them makes the exponents directly comparable:\n\\[\n2^x=2^{3(y+1)},\n\\qquad 3^{2y}=3^{x-9}.\n\\]\nFor a fixed base greater than \\(1\\), distinct real exponents give distinct powers. Therefore these equations are equivalent to\n\\[\nx=3y+3,\n\\qquad 2y=x-9.\n\\]\nThe second equation gives \\(x=2y+9\\). Substituting this into the first eliminates \\(x\\):\n\\[\n2y+9=3y+3,\n\\qquad y=6.\n\\]\nThen \\(x=2(6)+9=21\\), so\n\\[\nx+y=21+6=\\boxed{27}.\n\\]\nThe answer is D.","id":"common-bases-and-substitution","label":"Rewrite with common bases","legacyRouteId":"common-bases-and-substitution","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Accepted primary worked-solution review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/solutions-2026-11-06/1964_AMC_12_Q11.json","sha256":"27705b5a2c95e3792c580e4f07f1c62f1ebc6e5303d3887833f4c992ea653c16"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","reviewStatus":"AI-authored and independently AI-reviewed; not teacher reviewed","source":"Original Math Practice solution","sourceRef":"amc/1964_AMC_12_Q11#common-bases-and-substitution","sourceURL":"https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice"}},{"directNodeIds":["cnm_089"],"id":"substitution","label":"Equate exponents and substitute","legacyRouteId":"substitution","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Legacy graph and grading review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/solutions-2026-11-06/1964_AMC_12_Q11.json","sha256":"27705b5a2c95e3792c580e4f07f1c62f1ebc6e5303d3887833f4c992ea653c16"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","source":"Existing reviewed concept route","sourceRef":"amc/1964_AMC_12_Q11#substitution"}},{"directNodeIds":["cnm_090"],"id":"elimina